notes:
Sens now installs itself with its own installer, and the first time it opens it gets itself ready in a minute: your name, Claude Code, and what you already had in Claude Code.

### New
- **Sens's own installer.** The setup window is Sens itself: its type, its voice, and the stone of its icon, rendered live with its cut lit. *Instalar Sens* installs it in one click, or *Personalizar instalación* lets you pick the folder and the shortcuts. If Sens is open, it asks you to close it (and offers to force it after 10 seconds). The log says what it does as it does it, and on the last screen *Abrir Sens* opens it.
  It installs exactly where the previous installer did, with the same entries in Windows, so an existing Sens updates in place and Windows keeps seeing a single *Sens*. Uninstalling leaves your projects and their sessions alone, and only removes your Sens settings, skills and plugins if you tick that box.
- **A welcome that gets Sens ready.** The first time Sens opens, it walks you through:
  - your name, shown in the rail;
  - Claude Code: install it, sign in, or see that it is already connected;
  - **the Claude Code sessions already on your computer**, grouped by project: pick the projects and they appear in Sens, and continuing one resumes it in Claude Code with all its context. Nothing in `~/.claude` is copied or changed;
  - the MCP servers you set up in Claude Desktop, Cursor, Windsurf or VS Code, added to *Capacidades* and turned on in the projects you bring. Your Claude Code skills, plugins and MCP servers already work in Sens, so they are listed, not copied;
  - the project to start with.
  It shows once, *Saltar la bienvenida* skips it, and Settings › General opens it again. People who already use Sens will see it once after updating, since it brings the import.
